{
  "dom_id": "step-q115675",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/0d1dba03b8b14af89dbf06bf84f02c1a15f4f47f230900d4c46de87f14baeaf9.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/afb50fc9034d42ae57b9861dec4a2f5d0acf4f0c7122fc1ce845f5e3d60988fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =(1-x)(x-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $x=4$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<1$ | $1<x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the three remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{2}(1-x)(x-4).$",
